<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Bavaria-Munich</h1><p class="st-meta">1392 CE – 1505 CE · capital Munich</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Wittelsbach duchy centred on Munich that outlasted its rival Bavarian branches and reunited the country under Albert IV in 1505.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The partition of 1392 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-duke-of-bavaria">John II</a> the lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, the smallest share of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbach</a> inheritance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-bavaria">Upper Bavaria</a>. His sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-duke-of-bavaria">Ernest</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-duke-of-bavaria">William III</a> ruled jointly after 1397, and in 1429 they received a share of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-straubing">Duchy of Bavaria-Straubing</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dukes ruled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alter-hof">Alter Hof</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, a city grown rich on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trade</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-duke-of-bavaria">Duke Ernest</a> had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnes-bernauer">Agnes Bernauer</a>, the commoner wife of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-iii-duke-of-bavaria">Albert III</a>, drowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straubing">Straubing</a> in 1435. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-iv-duke-of-bavaria">Albert IV</a>, duke from 1465, built a stronger, more centralised state, and Munich's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frauenkirche-munich">Frauenkirche</a> was completed under his rule in 1488.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-iv-duke-of-bavaria">Albert IV</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-landshut-succession">War of the Landshut Succession</a> in 1505 and reunited most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a> decree of 1506 forbade further divisions, so the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> as its capital, remained united from then on.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-bavaria-munich.jpg" alt="Duchy of Bavaria-Munich" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_Bavaria-M%C3%BCnchen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1392 CE – 1505 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Munich</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ernest, Albert III, Albert IV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bavarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bayern-München</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1448&amp;focus=duchy-of-bavaria-munich" aria-label="Duchy of Bavaria-Munich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4949 493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Bavaria-Munich lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5269 749L5279 723M5269 749L5291 727"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5279 723h0M5291 727h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5269 749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Munich. <a href="/atlas?year=1448&amp;focus=duchy-of-bavaria-munich">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
